<!--
  COLLECTION track row, trailing action glyph.

  Frozen canonical: `Track Item N` > `Container` > `Button` > `Container` >
  `arrow_forward`, light node 2409:31572, dark 2444:18186. The instance is an
  `arrow_forward` at 24x24 and carries **rotation: 45** - the only rotation on
  either canonical page, and confirmed on all three track items in both themes.
  Its wrapper is 33.94 square, which is 24 * sqrt(2), the bounding box of a 24
  square turned through 45 degrees; that is the arithmetic that makes the rotation
  a measurement rather than a reading of the preview.

  So the arrow points north-east, not east. Figma's rotation is positive
  counter-clockwise and Android's is positive clockwise, hence -45 here for the
  same glyph direction.

  ## Which `arrow_forward`

  Unlike the empty-state illustration and the header overflow dots, this one is
  not authored from its box: `arrow_forward` is a named Material instance, so the
  Material path is the path, exactly as docs/COLLECTION-3.6.6.md took Material's
  `favorite` for the empty-state heart.

  That left one thing unsettled, and it was settled the wrong way until G-A7. There
  are two Material glyphs under that name - the legacy **Material Icons** one and
  the current **Material Symbols** one - and they are not the same drawing. Their
  bounding boxes inside the 24 box differ, which is exactly the evidence the
  canonical export does record:

    Material Icons (legacy)    16 x 12   at (4,6)    <- what this file used to hold
    Material Symbols           16 x 16   at (4,4)
    canonical `icon` child     16 x 16   at (4,4)    <- 2409:31572, and five more

  All six instances - 2409:31572 / 31560 / 31542 light, 2444:18398 / 18410 / 18422
  dark - report the same 16x16 at (4,4), on all four edges. The legacy arrow is
  16x12: a shaft with a small chevron, four units short of the canonical box top
  and bottom. So the family was right and the generation was wrong, and the frozen
  design has been drawn with a flatter, lighter arrowhead than the one it specifies.

  This is the same identification method docs/COLLECTION-3.6.6.md used for the
  heart - "20x18.35, which is the bounding box of Material's `favorite` path to the
  decimal, so the family is not a guess" - applied to the one property that tells
  the two arrows apart. The canonical exporter records no geometry for any node
  (see tools/figma-export/nav-icons/README.md), so a box that matches on every edge
  is the strongest evidence this repository can hold without an owner-run plugin.

  ## The path

  Material Symbols `arrow_forward`, weight 400, grade 0, optical size 24, whose
  source viewport is `0 -960 960 960`:

    M647-440H160v-80h487L423-744l57-56 320 320-320 320-56-56 223-224Z

  mapped onto 24 by x/40 and (y+960)/40, which is the standard transform between
  the two viewports and is exact here - every control point lands on a multiple of
  0.025. Extremes: x 4..20, y 4..20.

  Rotated 45 degrees about (12,12) the glyph spans 16*sqrt(2) = 22.63, centred, so
  it occupies 0.69..23.31 and nothing is clipped by the 24 viewport. The legacy
  arrow spanned 19.8 the same way, so the ring, its size, its position and its
  touch target are all untouched by this - only the drawing inside it changes.

  Tint comes from the view; the fill here is only what an untinted preview shows.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <group
        android:pivotX="12"
        android:pivotY="12"
        android:rotation="-45">
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFF"
            android:pathData="M16.175,13 L4,13 L4,11 L16.175,11 L10.575,5.4 L12,4 L20,12 L12,20 L10.6,18.6 Z" />
    </group>
</vector>
